1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,473
What if you hid ten thousand dollars under your mattress in the year two thousand, and didn't touch it for twenty six years?

2
00:00:06,793 --> 00:00:11,518
Nobody steals it. Nobody spends it. Every single bill is still there.

3
00:00:11,838 --> 00:00:14,752
You count it in twenty twenty six, and it's exactly ten thousand dollars. To the cent.

4
00:00:15,072 --> 00:00:19,960
And yet, it would have lost half of its value.

5
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:25,098
Today, three versions of you hide the same ten thousand dollars in three different places.

6
00:00:25,418 --> 00:00:30,306
One of them ends up with more than fifteen times the money. And it's not the one you'd guess.

7
00:00:31,106 --> 00:00:37,712
It's January two thousand. The world didn't end at midnight, and you just won ten thousand dollars in a radio contest.

8
00:00:38,032 --> 00:00:40,377
Everyone has an opinion about what to do with it.

9
00:00:40,697 --> 00:00:45,690
Grandma says: cash. Under the mattress. Banks can fail. Cash is cash.

10
00:00:46,010 --> 00:00:51,733
Max's uncle says: gold. Gold is real money. It's been around for thousands of years.

11
00:00:52,053 --> 00:00:56,802
And the man at the bank says: an index fund that tracks the S and P five hundred.

12
00:00:57,602 --> 00:00:59,634
So the timeline splits into three.

13
00:00:59,954 --> 00:01:05,364
Timeline A keeps the cash. Timeline B buys gold. Timeline C buys the index fund.

14
00:01:05,684 --> 00:01:10,560
Nobody adds a cent. Nobody sells. We just open the box in twenty twenty six.

15
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:15,582
And we'll use real historical prices for all three. Including the parts that hurt.

16
00:01:16,382 --> 00:01:21,325
First, a quick reality check. Why would cash lose value if nobody touches it?

17
00:01:21,645 --> 00:01:24,432
Because prices go up. It's called inflation.

18
00:01:24,752 --> 00:01:33,958
In the US, the consumer price index measures the average price of a basket of things people buy. Food, rent, fuel, haircuts.

19
00:01:34,278 --> 00:01:41,163
From January two thousand to September twenty twenty six, that basket became almost exactly twice as expensive.

20
00:01:41,483 --> 00:01:43,979
So the same bill buys about half as much.

21
00:01:44,779 --> 00:01:46,300
Timeline A. The mattress.

22
00:01:46,620 --> 00:01:54,341
In twenty ten, you peek under it. Still ten thousand dollars. But it now buys what about seven thousand eight hundred bought in two thousand.

23
00:01:54,661 --> 00:01:57,970
In twenty twenty, it buys like six thousand five hundred.

24
00:01:58,290 --> 00:02:05,279
And by September twenty twenty six, your ten thousand dollars buys what about five thousand and thirty four dollars bought when you hid it.

25
00:02:05,599 --> 00:02:08,641
You didn't lose a single bill. You lost half the money.

26
00:02:09,441 --> 00:02:14,746
And before anyone in Europe feels safe. The euro did the same thing, just a bit slower.

27
00:02:15,066 --> 00:02:22,253
Ten thousand euros hidden in January two thousand would buy roughly what five and a half thousand euros bought back then.

28
00:02:22,573 --> 00:02:28,169
And in many countries with higher inflation, cash melted much faster than that.

29
00:02:28,489 --> 00:02:34,514
Cash is great for emergencies and next month's bills. As a twenty year plan, it slowly melts.

30
00:02:35,314 --> 00:02:38,089
Timeline B. Max's uncle and the gold.

31
00:02:38,409 --> 00:02:45,596
In January two thousand, gold costs about two hundred and eighty four dollars an ounce. You buy a small, heavy pile.

32
00:02:45,916 --> 00:02:53,845
And for ten years, the uncle looks like a genius. By twenty ten, your ten thousand dollars of gold is worth about thirty nine thousand.

33
00:02:54,165 --> 00:03:00,911
By September twenty eleven, gold is over seventeen hundred dollars an ounce. Max's uncle buys a boat.

34
00:03:01,711 --> 00:03:05,345
And then gold does something people forget it can do. It falls.

35
00:03:05,665 --> 00:03:11,272
From September twenty eleven to December twenty fifteen, gold drops about thirty nine percent.

36
00:03:11,592 --> 00:03:15,272
Four years of watching your pile shrink. The boat is for sale.

37
00:03:15,592 --> 00:03:20,851
Gold doesn't pay interest or dividends. It just sits there and waits for someone to pay more for it.

38
00:03:21,651 --> 00:03:25,064
And here's a side story inside timeline B.

39
00:03:25,384 --> 00:03:32,744
In December twenty fifteen, after four years of falling prices, Max's uncle gives up. He sells all his gold at about one thousand and seventy six dollars an ounce.

40
00:03:33,064 --> 00:03:35,943
His ten thousand dollars from two thousand is now about thirty seven thousand nine hundred. Still a great result. He feels smart for getting out.

41
00:03:36,263 --> 00:03:43,786
Then, over the next ten years, gold climbs to over four thousand three hundred dollars an ounce. Four times the price he sold at.

42
00:03:44,106 --> 00:03:47,729
The hardest part of any investment isn't buying. It's sitting still when it hurts.

43
00:03:48,529 --> 00:03:53,614
Timeline C. The index fund. And this one starts with pain.

44
00:03:53,934 --> 00:03:56,000
January two thousand turns out to be almost the worst possible month to buy American stocks. Right at the top of the internet bubble.

45
00:03:56,320 --> 00:04:04,471
Then the dot-com crash. Then two thousand eight.

46
00:04:04,791 --> 00:04:11,617
By March two thousand nine, your ten thousand dollars is worth five thousand three hundred. Less than the mattress, in plain dollars.

47
00:04:11,937 --> 00:04:15,792
Grandma is not saying anything. But she's thinking it very loudly.

48
00:04:16,592 --> 00:04:19,448
It takes until twenty thirteen just to get back above ten thousand dollars. Thirteen years.

49
00:04:19,768 --> 00:04:25,550
But then the long climb begins.

50
00:04:25,870 --> 00:04:30,293
By September twenty twenty six, timeline C's ten thousand dollars is worth about fifty three thousand eight hundred. And that's price growth only.

51
00:04:30,613 --> 00:04:33,968
The dividends companies paid along the way aren't even counted here. With them reinvested, the number would be noticeably higher.

52
00:04:34,768 --> 00:04:38,263
Now let's put all three timelines on one chart.

53
00:04:38,583 --> 00:04:43,413
The flat line is the mattress. The amber one is gold. The teal one is the index fund.

54
00:04:43,733 --> 00:04:47,993
For most of these twenty six years, gold and stocks take turns being the hero.

55
00:04:48,313 --> 00:04:55,175
And the mattress never wins a single year against inflation.

56
00:04:55,495 --> 00:04:57,770
Notice something else. Both winners had long stretches where holding them felt terrible. Gold for four years. Stocks for thirteen.

57
00:04:58,090 --> 00:05:03,025
The people who got the big numbers at the end were simply the ones who didn't quit in the middle.

58
00:05:03,825 --> 00:05:06,228
So, September twenty twenty six. Open the boxes.

59
00:05:06,548 --> 00:05:11,842
Timeline A, the mattress: ten thousand dollars that buy like five thousand.

60
00:05:12,162 --> 00:05:16,853
Timeline C, the index fund: about fifty three thousand eight hundred.

61
00:05:17,173 --> 00:05:21,828
And timeline B, gold: about one hundred and fifty two thousand dollars. Over fifteen times the money.

62
00:05:22,148 --> 00:05:26,072
Max's uncle buys a second boat.

63
00:05:26,872 --> 00:05:29,659
So gold is the answer? Not so fast.

64
00:05:29,979 --> 00:05:37,235
Watch what happens if the radio contest happens ten years later, in January twenty ten.

65
00:05:37,555 --> 00:05:45,589
Same ten thousand dollars. Gold ends at about thirty eight thousand six hundred.

66
00:05:45,909 --> 00:05:52,956
The index fund ends at about sixty eight thousand three hundred. Almost double gold.

67
00:05:53,276 --> 00:05:57,177
Same three choices. Different start date. Completely different winner.

68
00:05:57,497 --> 00:06:04,138
Anyone who tells you they know which one will win the next twenty years is guessing. Some of them are just guessing very confidently.

69
00:06:04,938 --> 00:06:09,524
And that's the real lesson of the three timelines.

70
00:06:09,844 --> 00:06:16,706
Cash is not a bad thing. It's the right tool for emergencies and short term plans. It's just a bad place for twenty year money.

71
00:06:17,026 --> 00:06:22,773
Gold and stocks both beat inflation over this long period, but both had painful years where they lost a third or half of their value.

72
00:06:23,093 --> 00:06:30,268
Nobody knows which one wins the next twenty years. That's why many people spread long term money instead of betting on one box.

73
00:06:31,068 --> 00:06:34,237
So should you never hold cash? Of course not.

74
00:06:34,557 --> 00:06:41,639
Cash is what saves you when the car breaks down or the job disappears. Many planners suggest keeping a few months of expenses somewhere safe and easy to reach.

75
00:06:41,959 --> 00:06:46,254
The trick is to give every euro or dollar a job. Short term money stays safe. Long term money needs a chance to grow faster than prices.

76
00:06:46,574 --> 00:06:54,329
And if your cash is going to sit for a while, at least shop around for an account that pays interest, so inflation bites a little less.

77
00:06:54,649 --> 00:06:59,479
Grandma's mattress kept the money safe from thieves. It just didn't keep it safe from prices.

78
00:07:00,279 --> 00:07:04,540
One more thing. Remember, all of these numbers are in today's inflated dollars.

79
00:07:04,860 --> 00:07:11,791
In two thousand money, the gold timeline is worth about seventy six thousand. The index fund about twenty seven thousand.

80
00:07:12,111 --> 00:07:13,957
Still way ahead of the mattress. But inflation takes a bite out of every timeline, not just the cash.

81
00:07:14,757 --> 00:07:17,764
The honest part: this is not financial advice.

82
00:07:18,084 --> 00:07:25,259
Past returns don't predict future ones. Gold has no dividends and can fall for years. Stocks can crash by half.

83
00:07:25,579 --> 00:07:29,817
Fees, taxes and storage costs aren't included here, and they matter.

84
00:07:30,617 --> 00:07:34,843
Same ten thousand dollars. Three places to hide it. Twenty six years.

85
00:07:35,163 --> 00:07:42,187
If you had ten thousand dollars in two thousand, where would you have put it? Cash, gold or stocks? Tell me in the comments.

86
00:07:42,507 --> 00:07:44,457
And I'll see you in the other timeline.

